stance":0.6528,"shared":15,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"GB","distance":0.7533,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"CAR","distance":0.7819,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Ejiro Evero"},{"season":2025,"team":"LAC","distance":0.8273,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Jesse Minter"},{"season":2018,"team":"CHI","distance":0.838,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"},{"season":2023,"team":"TEN","distance":0.8484,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Rams went 10-6 in 2020 and outscored opponents 372 to 296. The defense did most of the work, finishing 1st in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 23rd in EPA per play. What set them apart was the highest singleback usage and the fewest defenders in the box in the league.","offense":"The Rams offense was well below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest singleback usage and the highest empty formation usage in the league. Jared Goff ran the passing game, with Robert Woods leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Rams had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 2nd or better in all five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per play, where it led the league, and it had no real weak spot. Aaron Donald was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Leonard Floyd, who had 10.5 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the fewest defenders in the box in the league.","special":"The Rams were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 80% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 15% of the money on the books. The offense also brought back more of its roster than most, with 91% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Jared Goff played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,952 yards. Robert Woods was the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 936 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Cam Akers and Darrell Henderson. On defense, Aaron Donald led the team with 13.5 sacks and Darious Williams picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Sean McVay called the offense as head coach and Brandon Staley called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Texans under Bobby Slowik and the 2025 Bears under Ben Johnson. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Packers and the 2025 Panthers under Ejiro Evero.","trends":"The offense went from 14th in EPA per play in 2019 to 23rd in 2020, a big step back. The defense moved from 11th in EPA allowed per play in 2019 to 1st in 2020, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was base defense usage, which went from 34% to 16%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2020. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}